Template: mqvpn-server/subnet
Type: string
Default: 10.0.0.0/24
Description: IPv4 subnet for VPN clients:
 The server gives each VPN client an address from this subnet. The subnet
 must not overlap a network that this host already uses, for example the
 LAN, a cloud private network or a Docker bridge.
 .
 The installation compares the default subnet with the routes and addresses
 of this host. If they overlap, it proposes a subnet that overlaps none of
 them, when it finds one.
 .
 This answer applies only when the package creates a new
 /etc/mqvpn/server.conf.

Template: mqvpn-server/hostname
Type: string
Default:
Description: Public hostname or IP address of this server (optional):
 The self-signed TLS certificate gets this name. The client command that
 the installation prints also uses it as the server address.
 .
 If you leave this empty, the certificate gets the name "mqvpn", and the
 client command uses the detected public IP address.
 .
 If /etc/mqvpn/server.crt and /etc/mqvpn/server.key already exist (for
 example from certbot), the package keeps them and creates no certificate.
 .
 This answer applies only when the package creates a new
 /etc/mqvpn/server.conf.

Template: mqvpn-server/port
Type: string
Default: 443
Description: UDP port for the VPN server:
 This answer applies only when the package creates a new
 /etc/mqvpn/server.conf.

Template: mqvpn-server/enable_control
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Enable the local control API?
 The control API listens on 127.0.0.1 only. It has no authentication and no
 TLS. Any local user can use it to add or remove VPN users.
 .
 This answer applies only when the package creates a new
 /etc/mqvpn/server.conf. For an existing configuration, run
 "/usr/lib/mqvpn/mqvpn-server-setup --enable-control".

Template: mqvpn-server/control_port
